`Vector3.project( camera )` works by transforming the vector through two camera matrices, chained via `applyMatrix4`:

1. **`camera.matrixWorldInverse`** — applied first, transforming the vector from world space into camera space.
2. **`camera.projectionMatrix`** — applied second, projecting the result into the camera's normalized device coordinate (NDC) space.

The implementation is in `math/Vector3.js` at lines 499–503:

```javascript
project( camera ) {

	return this.applyMatrix4( camera.matrixWorldInverse ).applyMatrix4( camera.projectionMatrix );

}
```

Because the two `applyMatrix4` calls are chained, JavaScript evaluates them left-to-right: the world-space-to-camera-space transformation happens first, then the projection. The doc comment on lines 493–494 confirms this intent, describing the projection from world space into NDC space.

The default value of `Info.autoReset` is **`true`**, assigned at line 32 of `renderers/common/Info.js`:

```js
this.autoReset = true;
```

When set to `false`, the application must manage its own animation loop and call `renderer.info.reset()` once per frame manually. This is documented in `renderers/common/Info.js` (lines 23–27) and demonstrated in the `WebGLRenderer#info` JSDoc at `renderers/WebGLRenderer.js` (lines 546–556):

```js
renderer.info.autoReset = false;
// ... after rendering a single frame:
renderer.info.reset();
```

The flag is checked in `renderers/common/Animation.js` (line 81):

```js
if ( this.info.autoReset === true ) this.info.reset();
```

When `autoReset` is `false`, this automatic reset is skipped, leaving the responsibility to the application. The `reset()` method (`renderers/common/Info.js`, lines 184–198) zeroes out frame-related metrics: `render.drawCalls`, `render.frameCalls`, `compute.frameCalls`, `render.triangles`, `render.points`, and `render.lines`.
